{#
  Post-quantum TLS on the management HTTPS server.

  /native/ip/http/secure-pqc-type

  One leaf, and Cisco-IOS-XE-http is a real module augmenting /native/ip, so unlike the SSH
  payload this one carries its own namespace.

  Same four enum values as the MACsec knob and the same meanings: pqc is pure (and fails
  closed), hybrid is classical plus ML-KEM, non-pqc is classical only, all negotiates the
  best available and quietly accepts a classical result.

  The shipped default is already "all", and 26.2 negotiates X25519MLKEM768 with a modern
  browser without any configuration at all. So setting this to all changes nothing on the
  wire; what it changes is that the posture is now stated in the running config instead of
  being an accident of the default. Set it to pqc if you want the management interface to
  refuse a classical client rather than serve it.

  Teardown removes the leaf rather than writing a value, so the box goes back to its own
  default instead of to whatever we think the default is.
-#}
<config xmlns="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0"
        xmlns:nc="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0">
  <native xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-native">
    <ip>
      <http xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-http">
{% if state == 'present' %}
        <secure-pqc-type>{{ pq_https_pqc_type }}</secure-pqc-type>
{% else %}
        <secure-pqc-type nc:operation="remove"/>
{% endif %}
      </http>
    </ip>
  </native>
</config>
